<% content_for :title, "Poker Decision Quality vs Outcome: Reviewing Without Hindsight" %>
<% content_for :description, "A good result cannot repair a weak decision. Review poker hands using the information, reasoning and confidence you had before the cards came." %>
<% content_for :og_type, "article" %>
<% content_for :structured_data do %>
  <%= structured_data_script(breadcrumb_structured_data([["Learn", absolute_url_for(learn_path)], ["Decision Quality", canonical_url]])) %>
  <%= structured_data_script(learning_resource_structured_data(name: "Decision Quality vs Outcome", description: "Learn to review poker decisions using the information, assumptions, reasoning, and confidence available before the result.", url: canonical_url, time_required: "PT12M", reviewed_at: @content_record.dig("domain_review", "reviewed_at"))) %>
  <%= structured_data_script(faq_structured_data([
        ["What is the difference between decision quality and outcome in poker?", "Decision quality is how well you chose given what you could know at the time. The outcome is what the cards then did. Because poker has variance, a good decision can lose and a poor one can win, so the result alone cannot tell you which you made."],
        ["Does losing a hand mean you played it badly?", "No. A single result is too noisy to judge a decision. Review the information, assumptions and reasoning you had before the cards were revealed, and treat the outcome as one more piece of evidence rather than a verdict."],
        ["Why record your confidence before seeing feedback?", "Confidence recorded before feedback can be compared with whether you were actually right. Over enough attempts that comparison shows whether your sense of certainty is calibrated. A confident wrong answer is the most useful signal of all."]
      ])) %>
<% end %>

<% evidence_coverage = "This review method draws on published research into retrieval practice and confidence calibration." %>
<article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-12 sm:py-20 lg:px-8" data-controller="lesson-session" data-lesson-slug="decision-quality" data-content-asset-id="<%= @content_record.fetch("id") %>" data-content-curriculum-id="<%= @curriculum_lesson.fetch("id") %>" data-content-concept-ids="<%= @curriculum_lesson.fetch("concept_ids").join(" ") %>" data-content-exercise-ids="<%= @curriculum_lesson.fetch("exercise_ids").join(" ") %>" data-content-version="<%= @content_record.fetch("content_version") %>" data-content-publication-status="<%= @content_record.fetch("publication_status") %>" data-evidence-coverage="<%= evidence_coverage %>">
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-sm text-ink/55"><%= link_to "Learn", learn_path, class: "font-semibold hover:text-moss" %><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span><span>Decision Quality</span></nav>

  <header class="mt-8 border-b border-ink/10 pb-10">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3"><span class="rounded-full bg-moss/10 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-moss">Foundation 03</span></div>
    <h1 class="mt-6 max-w-4xl font-display text-5xl leading-[0.98] tracking-tight sm:text-7xl">A good result cannot repair a weak decision.</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-ink/70">Poker outcomes contain variance. Review the action using what was knowable when it was chosen, then use the result as new evidence—not as a verdict that rewrites the original reasoning.</p>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs"><span class="rounded-full border border-ink/10 bg-white/55 px-3 py-2">10–12 minutes</span><span class="rounded-full border border-ink/10 bg-white/55 px-3 py-2">3 judgment checks</span><span class="rounded-full border border-ink/10 bg-white/55 px-3 py-2">Free · no sign-up</span></div>
  </header>

  <section class="mt-10 rounded-3xl border border-moss/25 bg-moss/5 p-6 sm:p-8" aria-labelledby="decision-quality-answer-title">
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl" id="decision-quality-answer-title">What is the difference between decision quality and outcome?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-7 text-ink/80">Decision quality is how well you chose given what you could know at the time. The outcome is what the cards then did. Because poker has variance, a good decision can lose and a poor one can win — so the result alone never tells you which you made.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="grid gap-6 py-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.15fr)_minmax(18rem,0.85fr)]" aria-labelledby="decision-quality-model-title">
    <div>
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-moss">Review model</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl" id="decision-quality-model-title">How do you review a hand without hindsight?</h2>
      <ol class="mt-7 space-y-5 text-base leading-7 text-ink/75"><li><strong class="text-ink">1. Reconstruct:</strong> position, stack, pot, cards, action history, and legal options.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">2. Record:</strong> your range assumptions, intended plan, chosen action, and confidence before feedback.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">3. Evaluate:</strong> compare the reasoning with an appropriately labeled rules, math, benchmark, or solver source.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">4. Observe:</strong> keep the result, but do not let one result stand in for expected value or decision quality.</li></ol>
    </div>
    <aside class="rounded-3xl bg-ink p-6 text-paper" aria-label="Decision and outcome comparison"><p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-moss-light">Keep separate</p><div class="mt-6 space-y-5"><div class="rounded-2xl border border-paper/15 p-4"><p class="font-semibold">Decision-time record</p><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-paper/65">Information, assumptions, legal actions, reason, confidence, and versioned feedback.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl border border-paper/15 p-4"><p class="font-semibold">Outcome record</p><p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-paper/65">Cards revealed, pot awarded, and what the result teaches only after the action was locked.</p></div></div></aside>
  </section>

  <section class="rounded-3xl border border-sand/40 bg-sand/10 p-6 sm:p-8" aria-labelledby="decision-quality-boundary-title"><p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sand">Evidence boundary</p><h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl" id="decision-quality-boundary-title">Confidence is a calibration signal, not a score.</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-7 text-ink/70">Retrieval-practice research supports answering before feedback, and calibration research motivates recording confidence. Neither proves that this exact poker lesson improves skill. High confidence can be accurately calibrated, underconfident, or dangerously wrong; it must be compared with correctness over enough attempts.</p></section>

  <section class="py-12" aria-labelledby="decision-quality-practice-title">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-moss">Active recall</p><h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl" id="decision-quality-practice-title">Protect the original decision from hindsight.</h2><p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-6 text-ink/65">Your first checked answer counts toward this session. No answer or reflection is persisted.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper-deep p-5 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_12rem]"><label class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink" for="decision-quality-reason">Before feedback, define a fair review.<textarea class="mt-2 min-h-20 w-full rounded-xl border border-ink/15 bg-paper p-3 text-sm font-normal outline-none focus:border-moss" data-lesson-session-target="reason" id="decision-quality-reason" placeholder="What evidence should be frozen before the result?"></textarea></label><label class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink" for="decision-quality-confidence">Confidence before feedback<select class="mt-2 w-full rounded-xl border border-ink/15 bg-paper p-3 text-sm font-normal outline-none focus:border-moss" data-lesson-session-target="confidence" id="decision-quality-confidence"><option value="1">1 · Guessing</option><option value="2">2 · Uncertain</option><option value="3" selected>3 · Reasonable</option><option value="4">4 · Confident</option><option value="5">5 · Clear</option></select></label></div>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-6">
      <fieldset class="rounded-3xl border border-ink/10 bg-white/55 p-6" data-answer="unresolved" data-concept="Result versus decision quality" data-correct-feedback="Correct. A loss describes this outcome; decision quality remains unresolved until the decision-time model and reasoning are reviewed." data-review-feedback="Do not infer decision quality from one result. Reconstruct the information and assumptions available before the cards ran out." data-lesson-session-target="question" id="decision-quality-q1"><legend class="px-2 font-display text-2xl">1. A carefully reasoned action loses this hand. What follows?</legend><div class="mt-5 grid gap-3"><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q1" type="radio" value="bad"> <span>The action was necessarily bad</span></label><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q1" type="radio" value="unresolved"> <span>The outcome is negative; decision quality still requires a decision-time review</span></label><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q1" type="radio" value="good"> <span>The action was necessarily good because it was carefully explained</span></label></div><button class="mt-5 min-h-11 rounded-xl bg-ink px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-paper hover:bg-moss disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-45" data-action="lesson-session#check" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper p-4 text-sm leading-6 focus:outline-none" data-lesson-session-target="feedback" hidden role="status" tabindex="-1"></p><noscript><p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink/65">Answer: the result is negative, while decision quality still requires review.</p></noscript></fieldset>

      <fieldset class="rounded-3xl border border-ink/10 bg-white/55 p-6" data-answer="calibration" data-concept="Confidence calibration" data-correct-feedback="Correct. A confident error is a calibration observation. It is not proof of low skill from one case, but it deserves review across repeated attempts." data-review-feedback="Confidence becomes useful only when compared with correctness over repeated, comparable attempts." data-lesson-session-target="question" id="decision-quality-q2"><legend class="px-2 font-display text-2xl">2. What does a 5/5-confidence wrong answer provide?</legend><div class="mt-5 grid gap-3"><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q2" type="radio" value="skill"> <span>A high skill score</span></label><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q2" type="radio" value="calibration"> <span>A calibration signal to compare across repeated attempts</span></label><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q2" type="radio" value="ignore"> <span>A reason to ignore confidence entirely</span></label></div><button class="mt-5 min-h-11 rounded-xl bg-ink px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-paper hover:bg-moss disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-45" data-action="lesson-session#check" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper p-4 text-sm leading-6 focus:outline-none" data-lesson-session-target="feedback" hidden role="status" tabindex="-1"></p><noscript><p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink/65">Answer: a calibration signal to compare over repeated attempts.</p></noscript></fieldset>

      <fieldset class="rounded-3xl border border-ink/10 bg-white/55 p-6" data-answer="reconstruct" data-concept="Hindsight-resistant review order" data-correct-feedback="Correct. Reconstruct the decision-time state, reason, and versions before using the outcome as additional evidence." data-review-feedback="Start with what was knowable when the action was selected; reveal the result only after that record is stable." data-lesson-session-target="question" id="decision-quality-q3"><legend class="px-2 font-display text-2xl">3. What should a disciplined review inspect first?</legend><div class="mt-5 grid gap-3"><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q3" type="radio" value="result"> <span>The winner and final pot only</span></label><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q3" type="radio" value="reconstruct"> <span>The decision-time state, assumptions, legal actions, reason, confidence, and versions</span></label><label class="flex min-h-12 items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper/70 p-3"><input name="decision-quality-q3" type="radio" value="emotion"> <span>How the result felt afterward</span></label></div><button class="mt-5 min-h-11 rounded-xl bg-ink px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-paper hover:bg-moss disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-45" data-action="lesson-session#check" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper p-4 text-sm leading-6 focus:outline-none" data-lesson-session-target="feedback" hidden role="status" tabindex="-1"></p><noscript><p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink/65">Answer: reconstruct the decision-time record before judging the result.</p></noscript></fieldset>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-8 rounded-3xl border border-moss/30 bg-moss/10 p-6 focus:outline-none sm:p-8" data-lesson-session-target="summary" hidden id="decision-quality-session-summary" tabindex="-1"><p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-moss">Session summary</p><h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl">The decision stays frozen; the result stays separate.</h2><p class="mt-5 text-lg">Score: <strong data-lesson-session-target="score"></strong></p><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-ink/70" data-lesson-session-target="missed"></p><dl class="mt-6 grid gap-3 text-sm sm:grid-cols-3"><div class="rounded-xl bg-paper/70 p-4"><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Your reflection</dt><dd class="mt-2 leading-6 text-ink/65" data-lesson-session-target="reasonSummary"></dd></div><div class="rounded-xl bg-paper/70 p-4"><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Confidence</dt><dd class="mt-2 leading-6 text-ink/65" data-lesson-session-target="confidenceSummary"></dd></div><div class="rounded-xl bg-paper/70 p-4"><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Evidence coverage</dt><dd class="mt-2 leading-6 text-ink/65" data-lesson-session-target="evidence"></dd></div></dl><div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row"><button class="min-h-11 rounded-full border border-ink/20 px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold hover:border-moss hover:text-moss" data-action="lesson-session#retry" type="button">Retry this lesson</button><%= link_to "Open your Review Queue →", practice_path(anchor: "review"), class: "inline-flex min-h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-moss px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-paper hover:bg-ink" %></div></section>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-ink/10 py-12" aria-labelledby="decision-quality-faq-title">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-moss">Common questions</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl" id="decision-quality-faq-title">Questions people ask about reviewing decisions</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      <details class="rounded-2xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper-deep p-5">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">What is the difference between decision quality and outcome in poker?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-ink/70">Decision quality is how well you chose given what you could know at the time. The outcome is what the cards then did. Because poker has variance, a good decision can lose and a poor one can win, so the result alone cannot tell you which you made.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="rounded-2xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper-deep p-5">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">Does losing a hand mean you played it badly?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-ink/70">No. A single result is too noisy to judge a decision. Review the information, assumptions and reasoning you had before the cards were revealed, and treat the outcome as one more piece of evidence rather than a verdict.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="rounded-2xl border border-ink/10 bg-paper-deep p-5 md:col-span-2">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">Why record your confidence before seeing feedback?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-ink/70">Confidence recorded before feedback can be compared with whether you were actually right. Over enough attempts that comparison shows whether your sense of certainty is calibrated. A confident wrong answer is the most useful signal of all.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer class="border-t border-ink/10 pt-8 text-sm leading-6 text-ink/60"><div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2"><div><p class="font-semibold text-ink">Where this comes from</p><p class="mt-2">The review habit taught here—recall first, then check, then compare your confidence—comes from published learning research.</p></div><div class="sm:text-right"><p><a class="font-semibold text-moss underline" href="https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1319572" rel="noreferrer">Retrieval-practice systematic review</a></p><p class="mt-2"><a class="font-semibold text-moss underline" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10459-023-10252-9" rel="noreferrer">Confidence-calibration study</a></p></div></div><p class="mt-5 rounded-xl bg-paper-deep p-4">That research is about how people learn, not about poker strategy. It supports the review habit; it does not make any single poker decision correct.</p></footer>
</article>
